45417] [17592186511387 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511387 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511387 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511387 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511387 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511387 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "96c86f48-8fdd-5608-aed8-3ae8938afdea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511388 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511388 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "96e90f42-a40e-5675-933f-77aa92860dd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511389 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "97e14268-ee5f-5656-86a1-d067419325eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511410 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511410 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511410 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511410 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511410 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3b3f22b-6d4c-5cc5-9130-cf5e72b4c48c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511414 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511414 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511414 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511414 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511414 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "baf6f008-070c-539f-b70e-a2e12580def6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511427 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511427 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511427 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511427 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511427 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cecc26af-aa31-562c-ae17-33a4a09196f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511445 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3f83acf-0c32-5e5e-8073-81cec7cfab01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511464 #edb/ref 1071 "An auto parts store sells $x$ units of a certain product at a price per unit $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $1$ and $6$ units of the product each day, how many units should the store sell to maximize its revenue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13ee04f2-9803-50aa-b0c0-1f54d4ebdeb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511464 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+63x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+63,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+63 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+21 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=3$ and $x=7.$ However, the value $x=7$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only new candidate is $x=3.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,3$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+63(1)+11=60 \\\\ R(3) & =(3)^{3}-15(3)^{2}+63(3)+11=92\\,✓ \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-15(6)^{2}+63(6)+11=65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the auto parts store should sell $3$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511467 #edb/ref 1071 "A private art studio can enroll between $8$ and $14$ students in a painting workshop. The fee that each student pays is $$72$ if exactly $8$ students enroll, and if more than $8$ students are enrolled, the fee drops by $$4$ for each additional student. Each student pays the same fee.\n\nHow many students must the workshop have to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize its revenue, the workshop must have\n{{field-1}}\nstudents." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ae7f739-7e2b-55d5-873a-c21108d944ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511467 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that if there are $8+x$ students, then the fee is $72-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(8+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of students}}\\times {{(72-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fee}} \\\\  & =(8+x)(72-4x) \\\\  & =576-32x+72x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =576+40x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the workshop can only have between $8$ and $14$ students, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n8\\le x+8\\le 14⇒0\\le x\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=40-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40-8x & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the domain $0<x<6.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ gives a maximum. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the workshop must have $8+5=13$ students enrolled to maximize its revenue." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511471 #edb/ref 1071 "A museum sells daily entry passes. The price of each pass is $$20.$ The cost of operating the museum includes a fixed daily cost of $$1,100$ and a variable cost of $$5$ per pass sold. If the museum can sell at most $300$ passes per day, what is the number of passes that maximizes the museum's profit?\n\nThe number of passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "244652f5-cacd-5369-8e7e-0fc003669027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511471 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ passes per day, the museum has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=20x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the museum is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{1100}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{5x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =20x-(1100+5x) \\\\  & =15x-1100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 300.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=300$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=15,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=300.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1100, \\\\ P(300) & =15(300)-1100=4500-1100=3400\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of passes that maximizes the museum’s profit is $300.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511475 #edb/ref 1071 "A hotel has $71$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $$50$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $$2$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance per rented room is $$4$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511475 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(71-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(71-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(50+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =3\\,550+142x-50x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(71-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =4(71-x) \\\\  & =284-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}-(284-4x) \\\\  & =3\\,266+96x-2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 71.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<71$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=96-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 96-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =96 \\\\ x & =24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=24$ lies in the interval $0<x<71.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=24$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=24$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=24$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit level when there are $x=24$ vacant rooms. \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. If there are $24$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50+2(24) & =$98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $$98$ per night." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511478 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511478 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511478 #edb/ref 1071 "A coffee producer company estimates that the profit of its coffee production, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-0.002{x}^{2}+3.9x-3,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ are the tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily. Given that the company can produce at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, how many kilograms should the company produce per day to maximize its daily profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "300985dc-198c-5051-b5a4-3809b4e56dd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511478 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x$ represents the number of tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily, and since the company produces at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<1\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-0.004x+3.9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -0.004x+3.9 & =0 \\\\ x & =975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=975$ lies in the interval $0<x<1\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=975$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.004<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=975$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=975$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $975\\cdot 10=9\\,750$ kilograms of coffee per day to maximize its daily profit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511480 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511480 #edb/ref 1071 "A clothes store sells $x$ hundreds of pants at a price $p(x),$  in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)=200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $500$ and $1\\,000$ pants per month, how many pants should it sell monthly to maximize its revenue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "397e9b41-cb22-58e3-8777-87698586bfce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511480 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ pants, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =200x+40{x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $5\\le x\\le 10,$  since $x=5$  corresponds to $5\\cdot 100=500$  and $x=10$ corresponds to $10\\cdot 100=1\\,000.$  Therefore, $x=5$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $5<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=200+80x-10{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-80x-200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2$ and $x=10.$ However, the value $x=-2$  is not in the permitted domain. Moreover, $x=10$ is already a candidate, so our candidates are  $x=5$ and $x=10.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(5) & =200(5)+40(5)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{5}^{3}≈1\\,583 \\\\ R(10) & =200(10)+40(10)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{10}^{3}≈2\\,667\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the clothes store should sell $1\\,000$ pants monthly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511484 #edb/ref 1071 "The manager of a bakery estimates that the profit of the bakery, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-2{x}^{2}+120x-1\\,500,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of loaves baked daily. Given that the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, how many loaves should the bakery bake per day to maximize its daily profit?\n\nThe bakery should bake\n{{field-1}} loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f837bd1-8f94-5b36-a5b6-46a5ca94e710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511484 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x$ represents the number of loaves baked daily and the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 400.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<400.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-4x+120,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -4x+120 & =0 \\\\ x & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=30$ lies in the interval $0<x<400.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=30$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=30$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=30$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$\n\nTherefore, the bakery should bake $30$ loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511501 #edb/ref 1071 "Upon launching a new online course, the chief financial officer (CFO) of a company estimates that its profit, in thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=0.3{x}^{3}-3.6{x}^{2}+10.8x+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the price, in dollars, of a single course. If the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$ at what price is the maximum profit obtained?\n\nThe profit is maximum when a single course costs $$$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "626a28da-89e6-52c5-8bca-1f554918b829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511501 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x$ represents the price of a single course and since the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$  the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 7.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<7.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8 & =0 \\\\ 0.9({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ 0.9(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=1.8x-7.2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =1.8(2)-7.2=-3.6<0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =1.8(6)-7.2=3.6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=2,$ the point $x=2$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2$ and $x=7.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =0.3(1)^{3}-3.6(1)^{2}+10.8(1)+5=12.5 \\\\ P(2) & =0.3(2)^{3}-3.6(2)^{2}+10.8(2)+5=14.6\\,✓ \\\\ P(7) & =0.3(7)^{3}-3.6(7)^{2}+10.8(7)+5=7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the profit is maximum when a single course costs $$\\,2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511517 #edb/ref 1071 "A school is organizing a trip to a theme park. The trip is offered to at least $12$ kids, and it can be attended by at most $20$ kids. The cost of each ticket is $$80$ if there are exactly $12$ kids. If there are more than $12$ kids, the price per ticket drops by $$4$ for each kid in excess of $12.$ Every kid pays the same amount for going on the trip.\n\nHow many kids must go on the trip for the theme park to maximize its revenue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83c84e0b-efbe-5ed1-89f6-e89dc6ca66e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511517 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that if there are $12+x$ kids, then the price per ticket is $80-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of kids}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(80-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per ticket}} \\\\  & =(12+x)(80-4x) \\\\  & =960-48x+80x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =960+32x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there can only be between $12$ and $20$ kids, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\le x+12\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=8$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=32-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 32-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=4$ lies in the interval $0<x<8.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=4$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=4$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=4$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the theme park should admit $12+4=16$ kids to maximize its revenue." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511520 #edb/ref 1071 "A plant spends $C(x)=150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousands of dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product per month. The plant sells the product for $$200\\,000$ per unit. Given that the plant can produce between $2$ and $9$ units per month, how many units should the plant produce per month to maximize its profit?\n\nThe optimal number of plant production is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "896ab313-4af7-5cfc-9d60-946032fe5a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511520 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product, the plant has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ thousands of dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+180x-150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $2\\le x\\le 9.$ Therefore, $x=2$ and $x=9$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $2<x<9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-48x+180,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-48x+180 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-16x+60 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =6,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the critical points $x=6$ and $x=10,$ only $x=6$ is within the permitted domain.\n\nAll that's left now is to check if $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =6x-48 \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-12<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at $x=6$ is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a maximum.\n\nOur candidates are $x=2,x=6$ and $x=9.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =(2)^{3}-24(2)^{2}+180(2)-150=122 \\\\ P(6) & =(6)^{3}-24(6)^{2}+180(6)-150=282\\,✓ \\\\ P(9) & =(9)^{3}-24(9)^{2}+180(9)-150=255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the plant will have a maximum profit level when it produces $6$ units per month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511522 #edb/ref 1071 "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511522 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(5-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(3+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $$6.7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511536 #edb/ref 1071 "A photography studio books $x$ photo sessions per day at a price per session $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the studio books between $1$ and $6$ sessions per day, how many sessions should it book to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize revenue, the studio should book\n{{field-1}}\nphoto sessions per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0e359a4-15e8-5a4f-83b1-ea0f9d507e49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511536 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+15x+45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-18x+15,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-18x+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ However, $x=1$ is already a candidate, so the new candidate is $x=5.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,\\,5,$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-9(1)^{2}+15(1)+45=1-9+15+45=52\\,✓ \\\\ R(5) & =(5)^{3}-9(5)^{2}+15(5)+45=125-225+75+45=20 \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-9(6)^{2}+15(6)+45=216-324+90+45=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize revenue, the studio should book $1$ photo session per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511538 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory spends  $C(x)=0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000$ dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $30-\\frac{x}{100}$ dollars per unit. Given that the factory can produce at most $2\\,000$ units per year, how many units should the factory produce per year to maximize its annual profit?\n\nThe optimal number of production is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7b5b31e-6d04-57eb-9051-cd4496c07c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511538 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per year, the factory has an annual revenue (income) of $R(x)=(30-\\frac{x}{100})\\cdot x=30x-0.01{x}^{2}$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =30x-0.01{x}^{2}-(0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000) \\\\  & =-1\\,000+29.7x-0.012375{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 2\\,000.$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<2\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=29.7-0.02475x.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 29.7-0.02475x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1\\,200$ lies in the interval $0<x<2\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1\\,200$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:   \n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02475<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1\\,200$ is indeed a  maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=1\\,200,$ and $x=2\\,000.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1\\,000 \\\\ P(1\\,200) & =-1\\,000+29.7(1\\,200)-0.012375(1\\,200)^{2}=16\\,820\\,✓ \\\\ P(2\\,000) & =-1\\,000+29.7(2\\,000)-0.012375(2\\,000)^{2}=9\\,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the factory will have a maximum annual profit when it produces $1\\,200$ units per year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511548 #edb/ref 1071 "The cost for a company to produce $x$ bicycles, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nC(x)={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+300.\n$$\n\nGiven that the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, how many bicycles should the company produce per week to minimize its weekly cost?\n\nThe company should produce\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles per week to minimize its weekly cost." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb428e79-eefe-5650-9407-e82260832c0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511548 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ represent the number of bicycles produced per week. Since the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 10.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $C(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+48,\n$$\n\nand solving ${C}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-10x+16) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)(x-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=8$ gives a minimum value of $C(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6x-30\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=8$ into ${C}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{″}(2) & =12-30=-18<0, \\\\ {C}^{″}(8) & =48-30=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive at $x=8,$ the point $x=8$ is a local minimum of $C(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=8$ and $x=10.$ Computing $C(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(0) & =300 \\\\ C(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+300=236\\,✓ \\\\ C(10) & =(10)^{3}-15(10)^{2}+48(10)+300=280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is the global minimum of $C(x),$ and the company should produce $8$ bicycles per week to minimize weekly cost." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511554 #edb/ref 1071 "A fast-food chain wants to open some new restaurants. The company estimates that its annual profit, in hundreds of thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-12x+20,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of new restaurants it opens up. If the company has a budget to open up between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, how many restaurants should the company open to maximize its profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7844b31-dcef-5082-a108-686c79e5bf57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511554 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x$ represents the number of  new restaurants opened, and since the company can open between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 8.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-{x}^{2}+8x-12.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-2x+8\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =-2(2)+8=4>0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-2(6)+8=-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=6,$ the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=6$ and $x=8.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+4(1)^{2}-12(1)+20=\\frac{35}{3}≈12 \\\\ P(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}+4(6)^{2}-12(6)+20=20\\,✓ \\\\ P(8) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{3}+4(8)^{2}-12(8)+20=\\frac{28}{3}≈9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the company must open $6$ restaurants to maximize its profit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511566 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511566 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511566 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511566 #edb/ref 1071 "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511566 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de3d69db-3561-564d-a214-9ac683352f20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511566 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $$\\,1296.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511575 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511575 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511575 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511575 #edb/ref 1071 "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $$700$ and a variable cost of $$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511575 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eefd8d60-720e-5861-a3ae-27d7c6377bff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511575 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ ride passes per day, the park has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=15x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the park is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{700}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =15x-(700+2x) \\\\  & =13x-700.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=500$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=13,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=500.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =13(0)-700=-700, \\\\ P(500) & =13(500)-700=6500-700=5800\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of ride passes that maximizes the park’s profit is $500.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
